  "question": "Consider the pytest test class `TestRankPlotScenarios` in the file `optuna_qa/rank_get_rank_plot_m4_dataflow/files/testcase.py`, run as `pytest optuna_qa/rank_get_rank_plot_m4_dataflow/files/testcase.py::TestRankPlotScenarios` (i.e. ALL test methods of the class, each identified by its pytest id `optuna_qa/rank_get_rank_plot_m4_dataflow/files/testcase.py::TestRankPlotScenarios::<method name>`). During this run the function `optuna.visualization._rank._get_rank_plot`, defined in the file `optuna/visualization/_rank.py`, is invoked (indirectly, through `optuna.visualization.plot_rank`). An invocation is one call of this function; consider EVERY invocation made by EVERY test method of the class, and aggregate over all of them.\n\nTracked variables (local names of the frame of `_get_rank_plot` itself), exactly: `colorbar_trace`, `colormap`, `figure`, `info`, `layout`, `log_range`, `params`, `scatter`, `sub_plot_info`, `sub_plot_infos`, `sub_plots`, `tick_info`, `x_i`, `x_param`, `xaxis`, `y_i`, `y_param`, `yaxis`. No other names are tracked (in particular not module-level names such as `go`, `math` or `make_subplots`, and not the comprehension variable `p`).\n\nDefinitions (defs): (a) the parameter `info` is defined at the line of the `def _get_rank_plot(` keyword, at the start of each invocation; (b) an assignment statement `name = ...` defines `name` at the line where that statement begins; (c) a `for a, b in iterable:` header defines BOTH loop target names at the header line each time a new iteration of that loop starts. (The function contains no augmented assignments; if it did, they would count as a use followed by a def.)\nUses: a use of a tracked variable is a read of that name in the frame of `_get_rank_plot` (as a plain value, as the object of an attribute access, subscript or method call such as `name.attr`, `name[i]` or `name.method(...)`, as a call argument, inside an f-string, in a condition, etc.). For an `if` the use is on the line of the condition. For a `for` loop, the iterable expression on the header line is evaluated ONCE each time the loop is entered (started afresh), so the names it reads are used once per loop entry, on the header line -- NOT once per iteration. The element expression of a list comprehension runs in its own frame and its reads are not uses, but the outermost iterable of a comprehension (e.g. the `xaxis.range` part of `[math.log10(p) for p in xaxis.range]`) is evaluated in this frame, so its reads ARE uses. Within one statement all reads happen before that statement's own definitions. A statement that spans several physical lines (a multi-line call, a `return`, a parenthesized expression) is attributed entirely to the line on which the statement begins: all its uses and defs are reported on that first line.\nObservation: one execution of a statement that reads a tracked variable produces exactly ONE use event for that variable, no matter how many times the name occurs textually in that statement (e.g. `f(k + 1, g(k))` executed once is one use of `k`); a statement inside a loop body therefore produces one use event per iteration in which it executes. Each use event is reached by exactly one definition of the variable: the most recently executed definition of that variable along the actual execution path of the same invocation (definitions never reach across invocations). That use event is one observation of the pair (variable, def_line, use_line).\n\nAll line numbers are absolute, 1-based line numbers in `optuna/visualization/_rank.py` as it exists in the repository.\n\nAnswer as a JSON object {\"observed_def_use_pairs\": [...]} whose list contains one object per distinct observed (variable, def_line, use_line) triple, with exactly the keys `variable` (string, the bare variable name exactly as spelled in the source, e.g. `\"foo_bar\"`), `def_line` (JSON integer), `use_line` (JSON integer) and `count` (JSON integer >= 1: the total number of observations of that triple, summed over all invocations in all test methods of the class). Each triple appears exactly once (the count carries the multiplicity, so there are no duplicate rows); pairs never observed are omitted. Sort the list ascending by `variable` (Python's default `str` ordering), then by `def_line`, then by `use_line`.",
